:**\n\nOkay, so I'm in the middle of this hand, it's Turn, and I've got a killer hand: Top Two Pair! Aces and Tens. Looking back, I raised pre-flop, then again on the flop. The board shows an Ace, a Ten, a Seven, and a Five. There's also a flush draw lurking with two spades on the board. My opponent has been calling all along, and the pot is around 1200. I'm up in this match, so I don't want to get reckless, but I need to build this pot. The question is: what's the best move?\n\nChecking would be too passive. It'd give a free card to their draws, and I'm not in the business of doing favors. I need to protect my hand and get value from weaker holdings. Betting small would be tempting, but I don't want to give them good odds to call with a draw. A big bet might scare off weaker Aces, which I do want to get money from, and is a waste of a strong hand. Given the situation, I'm thinking a medium-sized value bet, maybe around 50-60% of the pot. That seems about right. I have to extract value from them, while still keeping their draws honest.\n\nSo, I'm going to bet. A bet of 600 chips feels right. It's a solid amount, it puts pressure on their draws, and it gives me a good chance to get called by weaker Aces. It's a nice value bet, with a chance to get more chips later. That's my read. Let's see what happens.\n\n\n", 'output': 'Evaluation: You hold Top Two Pair (Aces and Tens), which is a very strong hand on this board ($A, 10, 7, 5$). The presence of two spades and cards connected to the 5 and 7 creates potential straight and flush draws. Your opponent has been passive, calling your previous bets, which often indicates a made hand like a weaker Ace or a draw. Checking would be risky as it allows your opponent to see a free card, potentially completing a flush or straight. A bet is necessary to extract value from worse hands (like A-K, A-Q, or a single pair of 10s) and to deny the correct odds to drawing hands.\n\nReason: With a strong hand like Top Two Pair, the goal is 