ent\'s action on Round 0 was 0, and that implies a call or check pre-flop. Let\'s assume they checked/limped. And I\'m in the Big Blind. Given the "On Round 0..." history, it\'s clear the game just started. Now, my opponent is sitting on a small stack as well.\n\nMy first thought is, this is an easy hand to play. With QQ, I want to build the pot. I definitely don\'t want a free flop. I know the valid actions are `[-2 (All-in), -1 (Fold), 0 (Check), 200 (raise the bet), or any value above 200...]`. A check is out of the question - that\'s leaving money on the table. Folding is ludicrous. All-in seems too aggressive this early, unless I\'m feeling particularly lucky/bluffy.\n\nThe key is this limp from the opponent. It screams weakness. They\'re likely hoping to see a cheap flop, and I\'m not going to let them. I need to punish this limp with a raise. Now, the prompt says the minimum raise is 200, but I don\'t want to give them an easy call. Typically, I\'d raise to about 3-4 times the big blind (BB), which is the standard play. I\'m assuming a BB of 100 since the minimum raise is 200. With a standard raise, I\'d bring it to 300-400, but I could go higher since the small stack makes more aggressive plays work better. I want to build the pot to get value out of this premium hand and drive worse hands to fold. I\'m going to go with a raise to 600, which should send a strong message and hopefully thin the field.\n\nSo, to summarize, I\'m playing it aggressively to build the pot and find out where I stand. I\'ll raise to 600.\n\n\n', 'output': "Evaluation:\nI hold Pocket Queens (QQ), which is the third-best starting hand in Texas Hold'em. The opponent has chosen to check (or call the blind), indicating a passive play and likely a mediocre hand. Checking here would allow the opponent to see the flop for free, giving them a chance to outdraw me or hit a lucky card. Raising is the superior option to build the pot while I have a significant statistical advantage and to deny t